<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> choose a snowboard jacket that's waterproof and breathable for your climate, with a fit that's roomy enough to layer and move, and features like a powder skirt, pit vents, and a helmet-compatible hood. Pick insulated if you ride occasionally or run cold; pick a shell if you ride often and want to layer.</p></div>

<h2>Snowboard vs ski jackets</h2>
<p>Snowboard and ski jackets do the same job. Snowboard jackets often have a looser, longer cut for freedom of movement and more coverage when you sit in the snow, while ski jackets often fit trimmer. Either works on a snowboard; pick the fit you like.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Waterproofing and breathability</h2>
<p>Many jackets list a waterproof rating in millimeters and a breathability rating in grams. Higher numbers mean more protection and more ventilation. Ratings are tested differently by different brands, so use them as a rough guide.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Dry, cold climates:</strong> moderate waterproofing is usually enough.</li>
  <li><strong>Wet or coastal climates:</strong> look for higher waterproof ratings and a quality membrane.</li>
  <li><strong>Riders who sweat or hike:</strong> prioritize breathability and vents.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Seam taping</strong> matters too. Fully taped seams keep water from leaking through stitching; critically taped jackets seal only the most exposed seams.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Insulated or shell</h2>
<h3>Insulated jackets</h3>
<p>Built-in synthetic or down insulation keeps things simple: add a base layer and go. Good for occasional riders, cold resorts, and anyone who runs cold.</p>
<h3>Shell jackets</h3>
<p>No insulation, just a waterproof outer layer. You add warmth with mid layers, so one jacket works from frigid January days to warm spring laps. Better for frequent riders and anyone who hikes for turns.</p>
<h3>3-in-1 jackets</h3>
<p>A shell with a zip-in insulated liner. Versatile and good value, though often heavier and less refined than separate pieces.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Get the fit right</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Room to layer:</strong> you should fit a base layer and mid layer underneath without the jacket feeling tight.</li>
  <li><strong>Range of motion:</strong> reach overhead and twist. The hem shouldn't ride up and the sleeves shouldn't pull back from your wrists.</li>
  <li><strong>Length:</strong> a longer hem covers your lower back when you sit or bend to strap in.</li>
  <li><strong>Women's cuts:</strong> women's jackets are shaped for different shoulder and hip proportions and often come in longer lengths.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Features worth paying for</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Powder skirt:</strong> an elastic band inside the hem keeps snow out when you fall or ride deep snow.</li>
  <li><strong>Pit zips:</strong> underarm vents dump heat fast.</li>
  <li><strong>Helmet-compatible hood:</strong> fits over your helmet for storms and windy lifts.</li>
  <li><strong>Pockets:</strong> a chest pocket for your phone keeps it warmer, and a sleeve pocket is handy for a pass.</li>
  <li><strong>Wrist gaiters or adjustable cuffs:</strong> seal out snow under your gloves.</li>
  <li><strong>Pant-to-jacket connection:</strong> some jackets connect to matching pants for a better snow seal.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Layering underneath</h2>
<p>Your jacket is only the outer layer. Pair it with a moisture-wicking base layer and a fleece or light puffy, and adjust the mid layer to the weather. See <%= link_to "what to wear skiing", guide_path("what-to-wear-skiing") %> and <%= link_to "how to choose a base layer", guide_path("best-base-layer-for-skiing") %>.</p>

<h2>Care</h2>
<p>Wash your jacket occasionally with a technical cleaner and reapply a water-repellent treatment when water stops beading on the surface. This restores performance and helps the membrane breathe.</p>
